          "ref": "Jeremiah 2:4-13",
          "refKey": "Jer.2.4-Jer.2.13",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "covenant",
              "idolatry",
              "judgment",
              "sin-and-fall"
            ],
            "image": [
              "water",
              "wilderness"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "lament",
              "prophetic-warning"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "Vanity — Idols. Vain - Fools; senseless as the stocks and stones that they made their idols of."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "Neither — They never concerned themselves about what God had done for them, which should have engaged them to cleave to him. Of drought - Where they had no water but by miracle. Death - Bringing forth nothing that might support life, therefore nothing but death could be expected; and besides, yielding so many venomous creatures, as many enemies that they went in continual danger of. No man dwelt - As having in it no accommodation for travelers, much less for habitation."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "My land — Consecrated to my name; by your idols and many other abominations."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "They — They that should have taught others, knew as little as they, or regarded as little, who are said here to handle the law, the priests and Levites, who were the ordinary teachers of the law. Pastors - Either teachers, or kings and princes. Prophets - They that should have taught the people the true worship of God, were themselves worshippers of Baal."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "Plead — By his judgments, and by his prophets, as he did with their fathers, that they may be left without excuse. Children - God often visits the iniquities of the parents upon the children, when they imitate their parents."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 10,
              "v_end": 10,
              "text": "Of Chittim — All the isles in the Mediterranean sea, with the neighbouring coasts; for the Hebrew call all people, that separated from them by the sea, islanders, because they came to them by shipping. Kedar - Arabia that lay east-southeast of Judea, as Chittim did more north or northwest; go from north to south, east to west, and make the experiment; look to Chittim the most civilized, or Kedar the most barbarous, yet neither have changed their gods."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "Their glory — The true God, who was their glory; and who always did them good, giving them cause to glory in him."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "O ye heavens — A pathetical expression, intimating that it is such a thing, that the very inanimate creatures, could they be sensible of it, would be astonished. Be desolate - Lose your brightness, as the sun seemed to do when Christ suffered."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 2,
              "v_start": 13,
              "v_end": 13,
              "text": "Of living waters — A metaphor taken from springs, called living, because they never cease, or intermit; such had God's care and kindness been over them. Cisterns - Either their idols, which are empty vain things, that never answer expectation, or the Assyrians, and Egyptians. Indeed all other supports, that are trusted to besides God, are but broken vessels."
            }
          ]
        },
